<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Romanos I Lekapenos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 870–948</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Romanus I Lecapenus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Romanos I Lekapenos was Byzantine emperor from 920 to 944. An admiral of humble origin, he became father-in-law and co-emperor of the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> and governed in his place. He made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 927, supported the eastern offensives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-kourkouas">John Kourkouas</a> and passed laws protecting small landholders. His sons deposed him in 944, and he died as a monk.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romanos-i-lekapenos.jpg" alt="Romanos I Lekapenos" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dumbarton Oaks · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Romanos_I_Lekapenos.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
